      "text": "Given the equations \\(x + y = 4\\) and \\(3xy = 4\\), we can start by solving these equations simultaneously.\n\n1. First, express \\(y\\) in terms of \\(x\\) from the equation \\(x + y = 4\\):\n\\[ y = 4 - x. \\]\n\n2. Substitute \\(y = 4 - x\\) into the second equation \\(3xy = 4\\):\n\\[ 3x(4 - x) = 4. \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ 12x - 3x^2 = 4. \\]\n\n3. Rearrange the equation to standard quadratic form:\n\\[ 3x^2 - 12x + 4 = 0. \\]\n\n4. Use the quadratic formula to solve for \\(x\\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{12^2 - 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4}}{2 \\cdot 3} = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{144 - 48}}{6} = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{96}}{6} = \\frac{12 \\pm 4\\sqrt{6}}{6} = \\frac{6 \\pm 2\\sqrt{6}}{3}. \\]\n\nSo, the solutions for \\(x\\) are:\n\\[ x = \\frac{6 + 2\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = \\frac{6 - 2\\sqrt{6}}{3}. \\]\n\nThus, we can identify the values:\n\\[ a = 6, \\quad b = 2, \\quad c = 6, \\quad d = 3. \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(a + b + c + d\\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{6 + 2 + 6 + 3} \\]\n\\[ \\boxed{17} \\]\n\nNote that \\( a = 12 - (6 + 2\\sqrt{6}) \\), although this format isn't what asked, it clarifies the initial mistake. But the two values and calculations for \\(a, b, c, d\\) must remain the same to get \\(a + b + c + d\\).",
