      "text": "**\n\n1. The given pairs are $$(x,y) = (2, 2)$$ and $$(x, y) = \\left(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{11}{2}\\right)$$.\n\n2. We start by finding the values of $a_1$, $b_1$, $c_1$, and $d_1$ for pair $$(x, y) = (2, 2)$$.\n\n$$2 + 2 = 4$$\n$$y = x$$ which simplifies to $$2\\cdot 2 = 1\\cdot 2$$\nSince, $$4 = 4$$ and $$2 = 2\\cdot 1$$\nWe have $$\\boxed{a_1 = 1}$$\n$$\\boxed{b_1 = 2}$$\n\n$$4 = 16 = \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{4}\\right)^2$$\nSo, $$\\boxed{c_1 = 8}$$ and $$\\boxed{d_1 = 4}$$.\n\n3. Next, we find the values of $a_2$, $b_2$, $c_2$, and $d_2$ for pair $$(x, y) = \\left(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{11}{2}\\right)$$.\n\n$$\\implies \\frac{11}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} = 6 = 6$$\nFor $y = x$\n$$x = \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{7}{5}\\implies \\frac{11}{26} = \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{11}}$$ To bring the radical into square form, take the square: $$\\left(\\frac{11}{26}\\right)^2 = \\frac{7}{11}$$ So, $$\\boxed{a_2 = \\frac{11}{26} }$$ $$\\boxed{b_2 = 7}$$ $$\\boxed{c_2 = 7}$$ and $$\\boxed{d_2 = 26}$$ 4. Finally, we find $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$ for the given pair $$(x, y) = (2, 2)$$ (the same values apply to pair $$(x, y) = \\left(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{11}{2}\\right)$$ such that we can sub them back into their respective expressions by taking their greatest common divisor: $$\\gcd(11, 26)^2 = 1$$ $$\\boxed{a = 1}$$ $$\\gcd(2, 7)^2 = 1$$ $$\\boxed{b = 1}$$ $$\\gcd(11, 7)^2 = 1$$ $$\\boxed{c = 1}$$ $$\\gcd(26, 26)^2 = 26$$ $$\\boxed{d = 26}$$\nTherefore, the sum of the integers $$a + b + c + d$$ is $$1 + 1 + 1 + 26$$ $$\\boxed{=>\\ a + b + c + d = 29 }$$\n\nThe answer is: 29",
